    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and left with lifelong seizures and visions by an overseer's blow, Tubman escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1849 and went back again and again to bring out her family and others.</li><li>Known as &quot;Moses&quot;, she rescued some 70 people in about 13 missions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>, and in the Civil War she served the Union as a nurse, scout and spy, guiding the Combahee River raid of 1863.</li><li>She spent her last decades poor in Auburn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, campaigning for women's suffrage and caring for the old and needy, and after her death she became an American icon of courage and freedom.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Tubman began her escapes on Saturday evenings, because newspapers would not print runaway notices until Monday morning.</p></aside>
    </section>

